1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
They attract.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
They repel.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
And they put a new spin on modern technology.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
From your car to your computer.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
From high-speed trains to high-tech space propulsion.

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Harnessing a fundamental force of nature as perplexing as it is powerful.

7
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, Magnets on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Welcome to Six Flags Magic Mountain in Southern California.

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Here, thrill seekers are taking the ride of their lives on the attraction called Superman.

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,680
A roller coaster that owes its extreme propulsion and popularity to magnets.

11
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:06,320
Six Flags wanted to build a roller coaster that went where no coaster had gone before,

12
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:08,700
and that was to break the 100 mile an hour barrier.

13
00:01:09,980 --> 00:01:13,580
In order to get the speed, with conventional coaster technology,

14
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:18,800
we would have had to build a lift with a chain probably close to 500 feet tall in order to get that.

15
00:01:18,801 --> 00:01:26,400
Ride engineers had to find a more innovative solution.

16
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:36,329
So we turned to magnets and electromagnets in order to generate an acceleration of the car that would break every record that was ever known in the roller coaster

17
00:01:36,330 --> 00:01:37,030
world.

18
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:42,900
Superman uses magnetic devices called linear synchronous motors.

19
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:48,420
Although the technology is complex, the underlying principle is elegantly simple.

20
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:53,800
Everybody remembers from when they were kids where you had a magnet with a north pole and

21
00:01:53,801 --> 00:01:57,800
a south pole, and if you took another magnet and got the two north poles together, they'd

22
00:01:57,801 --> 00:02:01,840
repel, put the north to the south, and they would attract each other.

23
00:02:01,841 --> 00:02:05,400
And that's fundamentally how this technology works on the ride.

24
00:02:05,401 --> 00:02:09,240
You have magnets on the car.

25
00:02:09,241 --> 00:02:15,700
You have electromagnets in the track, and the electromagnets can produce repulsive and

26
00:02:15,701 --> 00:02:19,780
attractive forces to move the car and as soon as the car gets to the place where

27
00:02:19,781 --> 00:02:24,100
it's being attracted the electromagnet changes its polarity the current changes

28
00:02:24,101 --> 00:02:27,740
and attractive force turns to a repulsive force and you keep doing this in

29
00:02:27,741 --> 00:02:32,940
synchronism with the motion of the car and produce constant motion

30
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:39,360
banks of computers and switchers shift the electromagnets polarity in

31
00:02:39,361 --> 00:02:42,020
milliseconds

32
00:02:42,021 --> 00:02:51,500
This enables the cars to accelerate from a dead standstill to over 100 miles per hour in just seven seconds.

33
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:57,640
To this day, it remains in the record books as the tallest and fastest roller coaster,

34
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:00,420
and we couldn't have done it without magnets and electromagnets.

35
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:06,980
We rely on magnets for a lot more than thrills at the theme park.

36
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:09,840
They're everywhere.

37
00:03:09,841 --> 00:03:13,420
playing a key role in virtually every kind of technology

38
00:03:13,421 --> 00:03:15,600
from our microwaves

39
00:03:15,601 --> 00:03:17,500
to our computers

40
00:03:17,501 --> 00:03:19,540
to our cell phones.

41
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:22,840
Throughout our house, throughout our car, throughout any office

42
00:03:22,841 --> 00:03:25,600
there are dozens and dozens of magnets doing their work

43
00:03:25,601 --> 00:03:28,740
but without being seen, out of sight, out of mind.

44
00:03:28,741 --> 00:03:32,940
We use magnets without hardly thinking about it every day.

45
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:36,480
Hidden on the stripe of your credit card

46
00:03:36,481 --> 00:03:41,460
are little areas of magnetization, like a barcode.

47
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:44,520
And when you swipe that through the card reader,

48
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:48,360
that barcode information is downloaded to the computer.

49
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:52,860
For many of us, our reliance on magnets goes hand-in-hand

50
00:03:52,861 --> 00:03:55,340
with our inability to fully fathom how they work.

51
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:00,840
Magnetism is fascinating because of the mysteries.

52
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:04,720
When you have things pulling at a distance

53
00:04:04,721 --> 00:04:06,580
without contact.

54
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:10,960
Certainly we understand when I push this hand against this hand

55
00:04:10,961 --> 00:04:13,040
where the forces are coming from.

56
00:04:13,041 --> 00:04:18,380
But when I take a north pole and bring a north pole close to it

57
00:04:18,381 --> 00:04:22,500
and they push apart, I don't see any contact.

58
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:25,160
And that is always puzzling to people.

59
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:29,700
A magnet is simply an object that attracts iron.

60
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:33,300
It attracts some of the objects that are similar to iron,

61
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:34,460
like cobalt or steel.

62
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:39,880
But it does not attract other things like wood, glass, plastic, copper.

63
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:43,620
So it's a very selective attraction force.

64
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:50,660
This selective force also has a handy knack for passing on its properties to the iron objects it touches.

65
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:54,880
This is a bar of steel.

66
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:57,800
Right now it's not magnetized.

67
00:04:57,801 --> 00:05:05,200
To show that this is not magnetic, I'll just simply bring it down to the pile of paper clips and nothing gets picked up.

68
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:10,400
All I'm going to do is touch the north pole of this magnet.

69
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:11,960
It doesn't matter what pole you touch.

70
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:18,760
Now, this bar of metal becomes a magnet.

71
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:25,160
A magnet works its magic by extending its force invisibly beyond itself,

72
00:05:25,161 --> 00:05:28,040
creating what's known as a magnetic field.

73
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:34,960
Now we can make that visible so you can see it with some iron powder.

74
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:42,140
And I'll cover the magnet up and put a white piece of paper on there so you can actually see it.

75
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:45,240
And I'll sprinkle some iron powder.

76
00:05:45,241 --> 00:05:49,820
These are little tiny particles of iron that will trace out the magnetic field.

77
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:54,380
It's collecting a lot of powder right between the poles,

78
00:05:54,381 --> 00:05:59,320
But looping out into space are these magnetic lines,

79
00:06:00,420 --> 00:06:04,300
returning to the South Pole, traveling through the magnet again,

80
00:06:04,560 --> 00:06:09,260
and exiting the North Pole in a circular looping fashion.

81
00:06:10,380 --> 00:06:13,540
To understand how a magnet is able to generate its field,

82
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:16,120
we must look to the atoms from which they're made.

83
00:06:17,900 --> 00:06:22,580
If you look at the model of an atom surrounding the nucleus

84
00:06:22,581 --> 00:06:25,940
in orbits are these electrons.

85
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:28,340
These electrons are charged particles.

86
00:06:28,780 --> 00:06:31,180
That constitutes a magnetic field.

87
00:06:31,380 --> 00:06:34,720
So atoms can be atomic magnets.

88
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:37,840
That's the reason something is magnetic,

89
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:40,000
because of those atomic magnets.

90
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:43,820
The paired electrons of many substances, however,

91
00:06:43,821 --> 00:06:45,380
spin in opposite directions,

92
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:48,140
canceling out their respective magnetic fields.

93
00:06:48,141 --> 00:06:57,020
Magnetism results only when more of an atom's electrons are spinning in one direction than in the other, free to emit their magnetic fields.

94
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:01,340
The atomic properties of magnets vary.

95
00:07:02,540 --> 00:07:11,840
There are so-called permanent magnets and temporary magnets, like the small bars on CD packages, which can lose their magnetism.

96
00:07:11,841 --> 00:07:16,840
To prevent one from triggering the security alarm after you buy a CD,

97
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:22,080
the clerk uses an electromagnet to neutralize or degauss its magnetic field.

98
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:26,220
And what exactly is an electromagnet?

99
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:32,020
Like the name implies, it's any magnet that requires electricity in order to function.

100
00:07:33,940 --> 00:07:36,540
Kids have been making the simplest versions for decades.

101
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:42,020
Just wrap some copper wire around a nail and attach the two ends of wire to a battery.

102
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:49,540
The electric current flowing through the coil creates a magnetic field, and the nail becomes a magnet.

103
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:53,780
Disconnect the wire and the magnetic field vanishes.

104
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:06,040
Perhaps no one values an electromagnet more than the junk man.

105
00:08:06,540 --> 00:08:15,700
At Columbus Auto Shredding in Ohio, magnets surging with electricity make quick work of

106
00:08:15,701 --> 00:08:20,520
scrap metal bound for recycling centers.

107
00:08:20,521 --> 00:08:25,860
This particular machine works with joysticks, right and left hand.

108
00:08:25,861 --> 00:08:31,540
And we swing the machine over the material, stop it, maneuver the boom into place, let

109
00:08:31,541 --> 00:08:34,060
the magnet down onto the material.

110
00:08:34,061 --> 00:08:36,200
We activate with the right hand the magnet.

111
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:37,880
We've got the material attached.

112
00:08:38,040 --> 00:08:39,940
We pull the boom back, pick it up.

113
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:42,000
We swing it over the truck.

114
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:44,360
Then we let it down into the bed,

115
00:08:44,900 --> 00:08:48,760
and then we use the left button to de-energize the magnet and let go of the scrap.

116
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:55,200
This six-foot-wide magnet, capable of hoisting more than 5,000 pounds,

117
00:08:55,400 --> 00:08:59,180
was crafted here at Ohio Magnetics just outside Cleveland.

118
00:08:59,181 --> 00:09:06,180
Its workers have been churning out king-size magnets for the steel industry since 1917.

119
00:09:06,181 --> 00:09:11,180
This is one of our Ohio Lodestar magnets.

120
00:09:11,181 --> 00:09:15,180
This is not a magnet unless electricity is running through it.

121
00:09:15,181 --> 00:09:20,180
The electricity comes in through the external magnet leads,

122
00:09:20,181 --> 00:09:24,180
goes into the terminal box, and down into the magnet coil.

123
00:09:24,181 --> 00:09:25,020
Magnet coil.

124
00:09:27,720 --> 00:09:31,880
Manufacturing an electromagnet this beefy requires several exacting steps.

125
00:09:32,840 --> 00:09:36,220
It all starts with a roughly cast carbon steel case.

126
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:41,540
This will house the coil that will carry the magnet's electric current.

127
00:09:43,700 --> 00:09:48,800
The first step is to flip the case upside down so a boring mill can hone its interior.

128
00:09:50,040 --> 00:09:53,120
This will ensure that the coil will fit inside it precisely.

129
00:09:54,180 --> 00:09:57,460
I have to check my measurements after every cut.

130
00:09:57,860 --> 00:10:01,720
That's to make sure that I'm not going over or I'm not undersized.

131
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:09,200
The most important dimensions to hold inside of this case here is the inside diameter and the center core diameter.

132
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:14,640
So the coil can fit in there properly and rest on the bottom and on these shoulders here.

133
00:10:16,620 --> 00:10:22,660
The precisely machined case is also tailored to fit the bottom plate of the magnet on which the coil will be wound.

134
00:10:24,180 --> 00:10:27,740
This is the bottom of an electro scrap magnet.

135
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:31,140
The bottom will get a layer of insulation,

136
00:10:31,860 --> 00:10:34,960
and then around here will wrap the aluminum conductor.

137
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:38,940
Thin strips of aluminum form the coil,

138
00:10:39,420 --> 00:10:42,140
tightly wound around a core fixed to the bottom plate.

139
00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:48,140
A thin strip of paper, called Nomex, is layered between each wind.

140
00:10:49,300 --> 00:10:53,000
The Nomex electrically insulates the adjacent layers of aluminum from one another

141
00:10:53,001 --> 00:10:54,300
to prevent shorting.

142
00:10:57,100 --> 00:10:59,140
After the coil assembly is complete,

143
00:10:59,820 --> 00:11:02,600
a welder fuses the case and the coil assembly together.

144
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:08,580
Then, workers pour in a proprietary insulating compound

145
00:11:08,581 --> 00:11:11,120
that will fill the cavity remaining in the case.

146
00:11:13,820 --> 00:11:18,000
The compound will prevent the coil from contacting the inner wall of the case,

147
00:11:18,480 --> 00:11:21,220
again eliminating the possibility of electrical shorting.

148
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,600
It will also act as a shock absorber as the magnet impacts against the material it lifts.

149
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:31,220
And it will help the magnet keep its cool.

150
00:11:32,780 --> 00:11:34,660
Heat is a magnet's worst enemy.

151
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:41,520
A magnet will lose about 25% of its lifting capability during a day's operation.

152
00:11:42,800 --> 00:11:49,540
The role of the compound is important because it transfers heat from the coil to the outside of the magnet case.

153
00:11:49,541 --> 00:11:53,660
and then air, which is around the outside of the magnet,

154
00:11:53,820 --> 00:11:57,540
cools the magnet to allow the magnet to efficiently operate.

155
00:11:58,540 --> 00:12:01,340
To cure the compound inside the magnet case,

156
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:03,940
workers transfer the assemblage into an oven

157
00:12:03,941 --> 00:12:07,980
where it bakes at 325 degrees Fahrenheit for 12 hours.

158
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:15,640
Then, after the magnet passes a series of tests,

159
00:12:16,060 --> 00:12:17,040
it's ready for action.

160
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:23,240
As we've seen, lifting magnets make all the difference at facilities like Columbus Auto Shredding.

161
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:34,200
But they aren't the only kinds of magnets the company relies on to extract the recyclable iron and steel.

162
00:12:37,300 --> 00:12:42,660
In its processing plant, where derelict cars have an appointment with Doom and the ravenous shredder,

163
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:45,980
a variety of magnets await the mangled remains.

164
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:54,040
It'll be their job to separate the iron and steel from the mass of debris.

165
00:12:54,041 --> 00:13:01,040
First, conveyors move the shreds beneath these rotating drums.

166
00:13:01,041 --> 00:13:09,040
Inside the drums, magnets set at fixed positions direct their attractive force down toward the passing fragments.

167
00:13:09,041 --> 00:13:16,040
The magnets draw them to the drum's surface, where they adhere until they're rotated up

168
00:13:16,041 --> 00:13:20,000
and out of the magnetic field's range.

169
00:13:20,001 --> 00:13:24,240
The shreds then fall to a conveyor.

170
00:13:24,241 --> 00:13:29,040
The uncollected shreds move to another magnet, set within the body of this rotating belt,

171
00:13:29,041 --> 00:13:32,320
to separate the iron and steel the large drums may have missed.

172
00:13:32,321 --> 00:13:40,660
The magnet is in the center, and what happens is material is processed underneath the magnet,

173
00:13:40,661 --> 00:13:46,380
picked up by the magnetic field, and then moved out by this conveyor belt until it gets

174
00:13:46,381 --> 00:13:51,000
beyond the field of the magnet where it is dropped off.

175
00:13:51,001 --> 00:13:56,320
On average, the plant's magnets separate 500 tons of iron and steel each day, which

176
00:13:56,321 --> 00:14:05,760
the company then sells to recycling dealers.

177
00:14:05,761 --> 00:14:11,400
At the junkyard and across the globe, magnets may not always attract a lot of attention,

178
00:14:11,401 --> 00:14:17,420
but their contribution is undeniable.

179
00:14:17,421 --> 00:14:22,000
One of the most ubiquitous varieties is hanging around in your kitchen.

180
00:14:22,001 --> 00:14:31,320
To make it, you have to shake, rattle, and roll.

181
00:14:31,321 --> 00:14:36,300
Many cows have magnets placed in their stomachs to attract nails, wire, and other accidentally

182
00:14:36,301 --> 00:14:40,340
ingested metals that could harm their digestive tracts.

183
00:14:40,341 --> 00:14:45,580
A magnet can safely remain there for the duration of a cow's life.

184
00:14:45,581 --> 00:14:51,180
Magnets will return on Modern Marvels.

185
00:14:51,181 --> 00:14:57,660
We now return to Magnets on Modern Marvels.

186
00:14:57,661 --> 00:15:03,620
In our world of technological wonders, magnets are the forgotten stars.

187
00:15:03,621 --> 00:15:08,820
Performing their vital functions so invisibly, we ignore their presence.

188
00:15:08,821 --> 00:15:13,940
And of all our unheralded magnets, the one we take most for granted is the enormous one

189
00:15:13,941 --> 00:15:16,900
right beneath our feet.

190
00:15:16,901 --> 00:15:20,480
The Earth is a giant magnet.

191
00:15:20,481 --> 00:15:25,380
An ocean of molten iron at its core generates its magnetic field.

192
00:15:25,381 --> 00:15:30,240
The field streams out at the North Magnetic Pole, loops around the planet, and returns

193
00:15:30,241 --> 00:15:33,480
through the South Magnetic Pole.

194
00:15:33,481 --> 00:15:38,060
This invisible force shields us against the lethal winds of radiation emitted from the

195
00:15:38,061 --> 00:15:42,220
Sun and distant stars.

196
00:15:42,221 --> 00:15:46,620
You can observe this drama in the skies above the Arctic Circle.

197
00:15:46,621 --> 00:15:52,100
The cosmic light show called the Aurora Borealis results when the Earth's magnetic field

198
00:15:52,101 --> 00:15:57,860
traps solar particles in the atmosphere and deflects them toward the pole.

199
00:15:57,861 --> 00:16:02,560
Closer to home, a more mundane variety of magnet plays a slightly less crucial role

200
00:16:02,561 --> 00:16:09,700
in our existence, as it clings to the appliance we visit about 11 times each day.

201
00:16:09,701 --> 00:16:14,840
Refrigerator magnets have enabled us to turn this nucleus of the American home into an

202
00:16:14,841 --> 00:16:18,240
information center and art gallery.

203
00:16:18,241 --> 00:16:22,400
Ever wondered where these indispensable little gadgets come from?

204
00:16:22,401 --> 00:16:29,020
There's no better place to start looking than FlexMag Industries in Marietta, Ohio.

205
00:16:29,021 --> 00:16:32,960
There's at least a 50-50 chance that the products on your refrigerator

206
00:16:32,961 --> 00:16:36,320
were made at this facility.

207
00:16:36,321 --> 00:16:41,460
FlexMag rolls out up to 100,000 pounds of magnets every day.

208
00:16:41,461 --> 00:16:44,580
From the strips that secure your shower door,

209
00:16:44,581 --> 00:16:46,620
to the signs you can stick to the side of your car.

210
00:16:48,180 --> 00:16:50,680
The two main materials that we use in our flexible magnets

211
00:16:50,681 --> 00:16:55,720
are synthetic rubber and then also ferrite powder.

212
00:16:56,160 --> 00:16:59,540
This material is made from strontium carbonate and iron oxide.

213
00:17:00,260 --> 00:17:04,400
This magnetic powder is only very finely magnetic.

214
00:17:04,800 --> 00:17:08,980
As you can see, it doesn't really stick to itself very well,

215
00:17:08,981 --> 00:17:12,060
so it has to be very fine and airborne before it will stick to anything metal.

216
00:17:12,061 --> 00:17:19,060
This is where the life of your fridge magnet begins, in what FlexMag calls an intensive mixer.

217
00:17:19,061 --> 00:17:26,060
After workers throw in the rubber, bags and all, the ferrite powder showers on top of it.

218
00:17:26,061 --> 00:17:35,060
We use rotors to distribute the material evenly, and then there's a large ram that puts pressure on the mix as it is mixing.

219
00:17:35,061 --> 00:17:40,060
Between those two, we get an even distribution, and we readily mix the ferrite throughout the rubber.

220
00:17:40,061 --> 00:17:45,270
the rubber. It takes approximately five to seven minutes to run a batch.

221
00:17:45,271 --> 00:17:46,500
Once the material

222
00:17:46,501 --> 00:17:52,540
is discharged from the intensive mixer, it's not in a format that we can use, so we dump

223
00:17:52,541 --> 00:17:55,562
it into what we call a batch extruder.

224
00:17:55,563 --> 00:17:59,380
We maintain the temperature of about 275 degrees

225
00:17:59,381 --> 00:18:05,560
through this process in order to keep the material so that it's able to extrude it easily.

226
00:18:05,561 --> 00:18:11,160
Next, conveyors transfer the extruded compound to other machines that granulate and cool it.

227
00:18:13,220 --> 00:18:18,320
Then the powdery mixture passes through heated rollers that press it into a thin sheet.

228
00:18:20,020 --> 00:18:23,940
At this point, this humble material wouldn't stick to your fridge or anything else metallic.

229
00:18:26,300 --> 00:18:31,160
But now it's finally ready to pass over the simple device that'll turn it into a magnet.

230
00:18:31,161 --> 00:18:39,760
It's a roller consisting of powerful permanent magnets classified as rare earths.

231
00:18:39,761 --> 00:18:46,360
These particular rare earth magnets are neodymium iron boron and are positioned such that their

232
00:18:46,361 --> 00:18:51,160
north and south poles alternate along the length of the roller.

233
00:18:51,161 --> 00:18:55,880
Before the sheath passes over this roller, the small fairy particles in there are really

234
00:18:55,881 --> 00:18:59,160
not aligned magnetically in any form or fashion.

235
00:18:59,161 --> 00:19:04,840
If they pass over this, the field that is produced off of this rare earth magnetic roller

236
00:19:04,841 --> 00:19:09,680
aligns those little particles in the sheet to create a magnetic pattern on the sheet.

237
00:19:09,681 --> 00:19:16,600
And those would be a mirror image of what this magnetic pattern is on this roller.

238
00:19:16,601 --> 00:19:23,820
Seconds after the sheet's bottom side is magnetized, a machine laminates its non-metallic top side.

239
00:19:23,821 --> 00:19:26,900
This is the side that will stare back at you from your refrigerator.

240
00:19:26,901 --> 00:19:30,400
emblazoned with everything from calendars to the phone number of your local pizzeria.

241
00:19:32,020 --> 00:19:35,900
But before FlexMag ships the rolls to its clients who apply those touches,

242
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:39,240
how can it be sure of its product's stick-to-itiveness?

243
00:19:40,740 --> 00:19:44,420
One of the ways we make sure that the magnetic material is actually magnetized

244
00:19:44,421 --> 00:19:46,400
is we use sheet vellum.

245
00:19:46,401 --> 00:19:51,760
The vellum is actually two thin pieces of plastic with a liquid medium in between

246
00:19:51,761 --> 00:19:56,100
that holds fine magnetic particles that align with the magnetic pattern.

247
00:19:56,101 --> 00:20:01,060
And this material will show you the magnetic imprint on the magnetic sheet.

248
00:20:01,061 --> 00:20:05,480
As you can see, it's the same magnetic imprint that we used on the rare earth magnet roller.

249
00:20:06,840 --> 00:20:11,880
Magnets have been leaving their distinctive mark on civilization for more than 2,000 years.

250
00:20:12,200 --> 00:20:16,540
And it all started with the discovery of a curiously attractive rock.

251
00:20:16,541 --> 00:20:27,340
Earth's magnetic poles have changed places hundreds of times over the past 400 million years,

252
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,060
reversing the direction of its magnetic field.

253
