256
00:20:50,860 --> 00:20:56,760
For centuries, scientists have attempted to solve one of the animal kingdom's most perplexing mysteries.

257
00:20:58,080 --> 00:21:03,920
How do some species regularly navigate vast distances across the globe without getting lost?

258
00:21:05,280 --> 00:21:08,320
Recent studies suggest an intriguing solution to the puzzle.

259
00:21:08,620 --> 00:21:16,620
Researchers believe some animals use tiny natural magnets in their bodies that enable them to sense the Earth's magnetic field.

260
00:21:16,621 --> 00:21:28,620
There is some evidence that birds and animals and fish use the magnetic field lines of the Earth to help them in their migration.

261
00:21:28,621 --> 00:21:32,620
They can find their direction, much like we do with a compass.

262
00:21:32,621 --> 00:21:39,380
Just as animals may use magnets to survive in nature, we use them to sustain ourselves

263
00:21:39,381 --> 00:21:44,860
in a world of mechanization.

264
00:21:44,861 --> 00:21:50,620
Our enduring relationship with magnetism dates back to antiquity.

265
00:21:50,621 --> 00:21:56,340
About 2000 years ago, the ancient Greeks and the ancient Chinese independently discovered

266
00:21:56,341 --> 00:22:00,140
Earth's only magnet crafted by nature.

267
00:22:00,141 --> 00:22:03,640
It became known as the lodestone, or magnetite.

268
00:22:05,640 --> 00:22:10,640
It's an oxide of iron, and the basic properties of the lodestone come from its atomic structure.

269
00:22:10,641 --> 00:22:13,640
But how it's magnetized is still a bit of a mystery.

270
00:22:13,641 --> 00:22:16,240
We think there was lightning strikes hitting the rock,

271
00:22:16,241 --> 00:22:19,640
and the magnetic fields from the lightning magnetizing the rocks.

272
00:22:21,340 --> 00:22:26,640
Chinese innovators used the unusual mineral as part of a device made of polished brass.

273
00:22:26,641 --> 00:22:33,520
On its surface, a spoon-shaped lodestone was free to rotate in response to the Earth's magnetic field.

274
00:22:34,960 --> 00:22:41,100
It marked man's earliest step toward the first practical application of magnets, the compass.

275
00:22:43,420 --> 00:22:50,020
A compass's magnetic needle determines direction by aligning itself with the Earth's magnetic north and south poles.

276
00:22:50,021 --> 00:23:04,021
If I place this in a little cup and allow it to float on some water so it's free to rotate, the compass will turn around and align itself pointing at the south pole.

277
00:23:07,660 --> 00:23:16,740
If I reverse the magnet, the compass senses that the pole has changed and points the other way.

278
00:23:17,360 --> 00:23:21,160
Reverse it again, it swings back around.

279
00:23:22,820 --> 00:23:27,600
By the 13th century AD, the compass changed the face of sea travel,

280
00:23:28,320 --> 00:23:32,060
relieving sailors from plotting their courses solely by the sun and stars.

281
00:23:32,061 --> 00:23:37,740
When storms obscured the skies, they could still ably navigate.

282
00:23:37,741 --> 00:23:45,300
In the centuries to come, the curious began to slowly unravel magnetism's mysteries.

283
00:23:45,301 --> 00:23:53,180
William Gilbert, physician to England's Queen Elizabeth I, led the way in 1600 with his

284
00:23:53,181 --> 00:23:56,820
landmark treatise, De Magnete.

285
00:23:56,821 --> 00:24:00,180
It was Gilbert who first concluded that the Earth was indeed a giant magnet.

286
00:24:00,181 --> 00:24:07,180
More than two centuries passed before an accidental discovery produced the next crucial breakthrough.

287
00:24:07,181 --> 00:24:17,180
In 1820, Danish physicist Hans Christian Ørsted stumbled upon magnetism's inextricable relationship to electricity.

288
00:24:17,181 --> 00:24:24,180
Hans Christian Ørsted was the first to demonstrate that electric current down a wire would produce a magnetic field.

289
00:24:24,181 --> 00:24:25,320
and magnetic field.

290
00:24:25,860 --> 00:24:28,140
He just had a compass needle near a wire.

291
00:24:28,580 --> 00:24:30,420
When the current went on, the compass needle turned.

292
00:24:31,180 --> 00:24:33,660
When the current went off, the compass needle turned back.

293
00:24:34,740 --> 00:24:36,760
And he thought this was very curious.

294
00:24:37,360 --> 00:24:42,800
This discovery tied together two separate disciplines,

295
00:24:43,180 --> 00:24:45,260
electricity and magnetism.

296
00:24:45,680 --> 00:24:48,720
Orsted discovered that they're really one and the same thing.

297
00:24:50,120 --> 00:24:54,080
A decade after Orsted discovered that electricity creates magnetism,

298
00:24:54,180 --> 00:25:01,180
English scientist Michael Faraday demonstrated the reverse, that magnetism can produce electricity.

299
00:25:01,181 --> 00:25:11,180
He found that moving a magnet near a stationary coil of wire, or moving a coil of wire within a magnetic field, induced electric current.

300
00:25:11,181 --> 00:25:19,180
The oscilloscope is connected to the coil, and it reads the voltage that is induced in the coil.

301
00:25:19,181 --> 00:25:25,360
So you can see as I move the coil through the magnet, the spot moves up and down in response.

302
00:25:27,780 --> 00:25:32,620
The faster you go, the greater the voltage.

303
00:25:33,460 --> 00:25:36,240
So if you need more electricity, you have to move it faster.

304
00:25:39,040 --> 00:25:41,060
Faraday's discovery was monumental.

305
00:25:42,200 --> 00:25:45,000
It paved the way for the invention of the electric generator,

306
00:25:45,001 --> 00:25:49,700
which enabled man to create electricity on a scale never before imaginable.

307
00:25:51,080 --> 00:25:55,180
The key to generating the power was to harness some form of mechanical energy

308
00:25:55,181 --> 00:25:58,420
in order to put the magnets or electric coils into motion.

309
00:26:00,020 --> 00:26:04,400
In a hydroelectric plant, there's water falling over the dam

310
00:26:04,401 --> 00:26:07,580
through the sluice way and turning the water wheel.

311
00:26:08,120 --> 00:26:11,020
In a coal-burning power plant, the coal makes steam

312
00:26:11,021 --> 00:26:13,720
and the steam turbine turns the generator.

313
00:26:13,721 --> 00:26:18,360
A wind power generator, the wind turns the blades of the generator.

314
00:26:19,340 --> 00:26:21,620
So the process is the same.

315
00:26:22,060 --> 00:26:25,180
Mechanical energy converted to electricity.

316
00:26:26,520 --> 00:26:31,320
In the early 20th century, magnets were also empowering the reverse of this process,

317
00:26:32,140 --> 00:26:35,960
driving electrical motors that converted electricity to mechanical energy.

318
00:26:38,120 --> 00:26:42,400
The motors had eclipsed steam engines as the era's prime movers,

319
00:26:42,401 --> 00:26:46,160
propelling everything from trolleys to cranes to elevators.

320
00:26:46,161 --> 00:26:51,940
In a common electric motor, an electromagnet called the armature

321
00:26:51,941 --> 00:26:56,500
revolves between the north and south poles of a stationary magnet called the stator.

322
00:26:56,501 --> 00:27:02,800
The armature rotates until its north pole is opposite the south pole of the stator.

323
00:27:02,801 --> 00:27:09,940
The direction of the armature's current is then reversed by a device called a commutator,

324
00:27:09,941 --> 00:27:12,940
changing its North Pole to a South Pole.

325
00:27:13,940 --> 00:27:17,940
The two South Poles repel each other, pushing the armature forward.

326
00:27:18,940 --> 00:27:21,940
The current reverses every half turn,

327
00:27:21,941 --> 00:27:26,940
keeping the armature in motion by frustrating its effort to bring North Poles near South Poles.

328
00:27:29,940 --> 00:27:34,940
Magnet-driven motors and generators accelerated the 20th century's modernization through electricity.

329
00:27:34,941 --> 00:27:41,940
electricity. Two inextricably linked industries powered one another's advancement.

330
00:27:41,941 --> 00:27:48,268
The rise of the electrical industry involved a great growth in the use of magnets.

331
00:27:48,269 --> 00:27:48,969
And

332
00:27:48,970 --> 00:27:52,212
also these things worked together. As the industry grew, magnets got better.

333
00:27:52,213 --> 00:27:52,913
As magnets

334
00:27:52,914 --> 00:27:54,053
got better, the industry grew.

335
00:27:54,054 --> 00:27:56,760
So it was very much a symbiotic relationship between magnets

336
00:27:56,761 --> 00:28:01,480
and electricity. Experts advanced both industries by developing

337
00:28:01,481 --> 00:28:06,780
new generations of magnets with ever-improving properties.

338
00:28:06,781 --> 00:28:13,700
The first commercial magnets were made from carbon steel, and later tungsten steel.

339
00:28:13,701 --> 00:28:18,580
These were stronger than the naturally occurring lodestone, but heat and jarring could significantly

340
00:28:18,581 --> 00:28:22,280
reduce their magnetism.

341
00:28:22,281 --> 00:28:29,000
In the 1930s, researchers created a stronger, more heat-resistant variety called Alnico,

342
00:28:29,001 --> 00:28:33,320
an iron-based alloy containing aluminum, nickel, and cobalt.

343
00:28:35,480 --> 00:28:39,960
Still widely produced today, they stored greater magnetism in a smaller package.

344
00:28:41,400 --> 00:28:45,480
But without an electric jolt, their magnetic potential remained dormant.

345
00:28:47,400 --> 00:28:50,040
You need a very large magnetic field to magnetize them adequately.

346
00:28:50,680 --> 00:28:54,160
So what you want to do is have an electromagnet with a large current.

347
00:28:55,760 --> 00:28:58,880
Magnetizing the material is a swift and simple process.

348
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:03,520
Just place the wannabe magnets close to an electromagnet, then turn on the juice.

349
00:29:04,060 --> 00:29:08,300
The intense magnetic field realigns the alloy's atoms to make it magnetic.

350
00:29:14,080 --> 00:29:20,820
In the 1950s, Alnico magnets were joined by low-cost compact alternatives called ferrites.

351
00:29:22,100 --> 00:29:27,220
Today, these iron oxide compounds account for about 90% of the permanent magnet market.

352
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:35,000
The ferrite magnets allowed motors to be made in a smaller diameter, shorter length,

353
00:29:35,001 --> 00:29:40,000
and really the application fitted itself very well for the automobile industry.

354
00:29:40,001 --> 00:29:46,000
Now you could have seat positioner motors, for example, that fit very nicely beneath the passenger seat,

355
00:29:46,001 --> 00:29:52,000
and window lift motors that were now small enough to fit very nicely into the door of an automobile.

356
00:29:52,001 --> 00:29:57,000
Today, magnets play a key role in not only our cars,

357
00:29:57,001 --> 00:30:00,000
but also our computers.

358
00:30:00,001 --> 00:30:06,000
Researchers exploring new frontiers in that field are beginning to see the light.

359
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:17,000
The most powerful known magnetic object in the universe is a neutron star, SGR 180620,

360
00:30:17,001 --> 00:30:20,000
in the constellation of Sagittarius.

361
00:30:20,001 --> 00:30:25,000
Its magnetic field is a thousand trillion times stronger than Earth's.

